    "C5": {
      "severity": "PASS",
      "supports": "USPTO ODP returns 4 Eve UAM applications pre-cutoff: 2 GRANTED utility/design VTOL patents (US 11,643,199 'Vertical Take-Off and Landing (VTOL) Aircraft' granted 2023-05-09 with rich CPC coverage in B64C 27/26, B64C 29/0025, B64C 39/08; and design patent D1115657 'Vertical Takeoff and Landing (VTOL) Vehicle') plus 2 provisional applications from 2023 ('Integrated Aircraft Command' and 'Flight Control Command Source Management') \u2014 both UATM / flight-control-relevant. Embraer S.A. has 162 granted US patents pre-cutoff including aviation-relevant work (e.g. 'Autonomous Unit for Emergency Lighting System for Aircraft, eVTOLs, VTOLs and Rotorcraft' explicitly mentions eVTOL).",
      "M_check": "uspto_odp.query_assignee for 'Eve UAM', 'Eve Holding', 'Embraer'",
      "M_value": "Eve UAM: n_granted=2 (both VTOL-classified, evtol category=2), n_total=4 with 2 provisionals in flight-control. Eve Holding: 0. Embraer: n_granted=162 with explicit eVTOL-titled grants.",
      "interpretation": "Eve's own granted-patent count is small (2) \u2014 consistent with a company spun out from a parent and operating only since 2022. But the patents that exist are squarely on-topic (VTOL aircraft architecture). Combined with Embraer's 162-patent aerospace estate \u2014 to which Eve has explicit royalty-free access under the MSA \u2014 the IP-portfolio claim is supported. No 'Nikola pattern' here (where the company claims 51 patents but 0 are in claimed categories). PASS, though Eve's standalone IP is modest and the substantive portfolio depth depends on the Embraer license."
    },
    "C6": {
      "severity": "MODERATE_UNDERDELIVERY",
      "supports": "Cannot independently verify the EAH loan via EDGAR (Embraer FPI structurally not searchable per C4 result). However the disclosure itself surfaces several issues worth flagging from the slice: (a) Eve has lent $83.1M of its own cash UPSTREAM to its parent's US subsidiary at 5.97% \u2014 at a moment when Eve is pre-revenue and burning $94.5M/yr; (b) the maturity is August 2024, just two months after our 2024-06-30 cutoff, which is a near-term liquidity inflection; (c) FY2023 cash burn $94.5M vs. cash $46.9M + securities $111.2M + EAH receivable $83.1M + undrawn BNDES $75.1M = ~$316M total liquidity \u2014 runway ~3.3yrs at FY23 burn rate, but only if EAH actually returns the $83.1M on schedule.",
      "M_check": "edgar_fts.query_fulltext cik=0001355856 ('EVE UAM'); already 0 (structurally non-indexed) \u2014 see C4.",
      "M_value": "0 hits but structurally inconclusive. Self-disclosed: receivable from related party = $83.1M; debt drawn $26.1M of $101.2M BNDES; net loss $126M; net operating cash use $94.5M.",
      "interpretation": "The structural fact pattern \u2014 cash-poor pre-revenue subsidiary financing its parent's affiliate at 5.97% with maturity right at the cutoff \u2014 is a moderate yellow flag. Not a Nikola-style fabrication (the loan exists and was disclosed in 8-K Jan 2023). But it weakens the 'twelve-months-of-liquidity' assertion because liquidity is contingent on EAH timely repayment. MODERATE_UNDERDELIVERY because management's standalone liquidity story relies on a related-party receivable they cannot independently enforce, and Embraer's FPI status means external monitoring is impaired."
    },
